.TH ASCII85 1 "August 2026" "ascii85 2.1.0-a85x" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ascii85 \- encode or decode Ascii85 and A85X1 data
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ascii85
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.RI [ FILE ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ascii85
encodes FILE, or standard input, as classic Ascii85 by default.
Use
.B \-d
to decode.
Use
.B \-x
or
.B \-\-a85x
for the canonical A85X1 envelope.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-decode
Decode instead of encode.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-a85x
Use A85X1 mode.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-ignore\-garbage
Ascii85 decode only: skip invalid non-whitespace characters.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-delims
Ascii85 only: omit delimiters when encoding, or do not require them when decoding.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-wrap =COLS
Ascii85 encode only: wrap encoded output after COLS characters. Use 0 to disable.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-y\-abbr
Ascii85 only: abbreviate four spaces as y.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help.
.SH A85X1
A85X1 uses the envelope:
.PP
.B A85X1:<payload>:<pad>:<CRC32>
.PP
The decoder is strict. It rejects trailing bytes, embedded invalid characters,
non-canonical checksum formatting, invalid padding, out-of-range radix-85
groups, and CRC mismatches. Decoded bytes are held in a temporary stream until
the checksum succeeds, so unverified output is not released to stdout.
.PP
CRC-32 is for accidental corruption detection only and is not authentication.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero on success, non-zero on invalid input, I/O errors, or invalid options.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR base64 (1)
